Yes, this setup is correct. Different shards and clusters will use different files in a bucket.

Using a shared bucket has numerous advantages:
- the underlying storage of the S3 bucket is incrementally scaled and partitioned depending on the load, and having a single large bucket will have better performance as it will be scaled for the aggregate workload;
- you don't face a limit on the maximum number of S3 buckets per account;
- you have a simpler configuration.

If you put different data in the same path, it will be difficult to delete the data related to a single cluster, and it will be impossible to configure different access rules. That's why I recommend putting the data for different clusters into different prefixes in a single bucket.

In most cases, I recommend using a shared bucket.

But there are rare scenarios when you can prefer multiple buckets:
- the logical separation to different buckets looks cleaner;
- it's easier to specify intelligent tiering separately for different clusters;
- it's easier to account costs or to manage the buckets as an isolated entity;

Often, it means that you don't have enough memory on the machines, and you should scale them up.

But you can also configure your query for lower memory usage by using these two settings:
- `max_threads`: lower the number of query processing threads, and it will lower the memory usage proportionally in the expense of speed;
- `max_block_size`: lower it a few times from the default value, and it will also lower the memory usage proportionally in some expense of speed;

