A few hypothesis on which resources there might be a competition : 
* Reading the local table in order to copy it before joining (`GLOBAL` behavior) : no, as I understand, there is no locking when reading from such table ;
* Writing the local table to the target (still `GLOBAL` behavior) : well, maybe as they are all the same, they share the same name. However, I have been able to test with different tables and obtained the same result, so no, it's not that ;
* CPU for query execution : those servers receive hundreds of request at the same time, they should be able to handle a few more without such a large impact ;
* I/O to send the local table to the replicas (in case all requests share one link) : that's my strongest guess, but I'm not sure ;

I may be missing something obvious. Any insights ?

I'm back with some news. By modifying the way I was generating my requests, I was able to reduce their execution time. From around 40-60 seconds to half a second (when multi-threaded). 

The 3 main improvements are : 
* Don't cast fields in the `JOIN` `ON` condition ;
* Don't perform multiple `UNION` on the same table while you could select all the fields into one `SELECT` ;
* Add a `WHERE` condition, similar to the `JOIN` to filter the data table before performing the `JOIN` ;

Doing so doesn't solve the original problem, the request still run twice slower when in parallel, which is **expected**. These enhancements help to mitigate that though.

Appreciate the help !

