This issue is opened during code walk through for many "Session expired" error logs from zkutil::EphemeralNodeHolder::~EphemeralNodeHolder(). Not easy to reproduce it, but the logic usage of pointer and reference is enough to prove there is a potential bug in the usage of shared zookeeper for ReplicatedMergeTree.

@alexey-milovidov Please help to confirm if this is a potential bug for zookeeper or not. CH do automically establish a new session with ZooKeeper, but some underlying class cannot see or use it, but only the old expired one.

Following are the details to describe the problem:
1. From server's error log, we can see huge log entries for "Session expired" from EphemeralNodeHolder. The during time is about 3 hours, and there is no readonly table.

2. Then I tried to dig on the backtrace, and did some code walkthrough.
1). From comment in src/Common/ZooKeeper/ZooKeeperImpl.h, if a zookeeper object is expired, we can only destroy it. This means that if the session is expired, we need to start a new zookeeper session, while a new ZooKeeper object will be returned for future usage.

    /// If expired, you can only destroy the object. All other methods will throw exception.
    bool isExpired() const override { return expired; }

2). But in EphemeralNodeHolder class, we use object reference for zookeeper, “ZooKeeper & zookeeper_”. As I know, this reference is for the current zookeeper, if it is expired, then will be discard, since a new ZooKeeper object will be constructed.
Hence, I guess the huge expired error logs were due to old zookeeper object has expired, no reconnect inside it.
...
EphemeralNodeHolder(const std::string & path_, ZooKeeper & zookeeper_, bool create, bool sequential, const std::string & data) <<<<< wrong use of reference for ZooKeeper &.
            : path(path_), zookeeper(zookeeper_)
    {
        if (create)
            path = zookeeper.create(path, data, sequential ? CreateMode::EphemeralSequential : CreateMode::Ephemeral);
    }
...

3).  The ZooKeeper object reference is passed from zkutil::LeaderElection() which is initialized in StorageReplicatedMergeTree::enterLeaderElection().

zkutil::ZooKeeperPtr current_zookeeper;        /// Use only the methods below.

void StorageReplicatedMergeTree::enterLeaderElection()
{
...
    try
    {
        leader_election = std::make_shared<zkutil::LeaderElection>(
            getContext()->getSchedulePool(),
            fs::path(zookeeper_path) / "leader_election",
            *current_zookeeper,    /// current_zookeeper lives for the lifetime of leader_election, **<<<< Here a pointer is changed to an object which the pointer pointed**
                                   ///  since before changing `current_zookeeper`, `leader_election` object is destroyed in `partialShutdown` method.
            callback,
            replica_name);
    }
...

    LeaderElection(
        DB::BackgroundSchedulePool & pool_,
        const std::string & path_,
        **ZooKeeper & zookeeper_**,  <<<< object reference
...

4).  When the session expire happens, the contex will start a new session. Then ReplicatedMergeTreeRestartingThread::run() will call setZooKeeper() to set the pointer current_zookeeper to the new one. The old *current_zookeeper is just left there, referenced by underlying class like LeaderElection.

src/Common/ZooKeeper/ZooKeeper.h
